Uh, looking through those, I think it's obvious in some cases there's some

01:15:51.680 --> 01:15:55.600
confusion around what's being proposed, and I wanna try and help resolve

01:15:55.600 --> 01:15:59.320
that confusion as best we can because so some of it is explaining what this

01:15:59.320 --> 01:16:03.130
is not as much as what it is, um, and so this,

01:16:03.320 --> 01:16:06.510
uh, is, is related to the, the depot property.

01:16:06.900 --> 01:16:08.610
As you know, and you've taken some other actions,

01:16:08.660 --> 01:16:11.340
uh, recently on, on other components of the property,

01:16:11.390 --> 01:16:14.180
so I wanna kinda go through that, that bigger picture so we understand and

01:16:14.180 --> 01:16:15.660
kinda pull, pull these things apart.

01:16:15.700 --> 01:16:18.740
There are some cases where we've had some questions and concerns that are

01:16:18.740 --> 01:16:20.220
really kind of mixing apples and oranges,

01:16:20.260 --> 01:16:21.940
and I wanna kinda clear that up if we can.

01:16:22.760 --> 01:16:26.640
So, uh, as, as you know, uh, in the recent past,

01:16:26.720 --> 01:16:29.400
the depot property came back under our direct control.

01:16:29.480 --> 01:16:31.790
It's, it's been a city owned property but had been,

01:16:32.040 --> 01:16:35.710
uh, allocated to the Early Works Museum for its management and use for,

01:16:35.800 --> 01:16:39.870
for quite a few years, uh, and so it's come back to us and we began a process

01:16:39.880 --> 01:16:42.880
of kind of looking at what are the various uses and things that we could

01:16:42.880 --> 01:16:44.720
do, uh, with the property.

01:16:45.250 --> 01:16:49.850
Um, we've had, uh, a handful of public meetings as well as just in some

01:16:49.850 --> 01:16:53.250
cases just email, uh, input and things we received from different citizens

01:16:53.250 --> 01:16:55.290
on things, but one of the things I would tell,

01:16:55.460 --> 01:16:59.730
uh, folks is, uh, we have a web page on, on the city website that gives

01:16:59.730 --> 01:17:02.690
a lot of information and gives results of a lot of the surveys and things

01:17:02.690 --> 01:17:05.450
that, uh, that were conducted by the planning department at some of those

01:17:05.450 --> 01:17:06.970
public hearings and so there's good data there.

01:17:07.480 --> 01:17:11.380
But in general, the things that, uh, that we heard from the public,

01:17:11.650 --> 01:17:14.900
uh, and then also coupled with what our own,

01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:17.640
so our own departments like Parks and Rec and others that engage with the

01:17:17.640 --> 01:17:20.630
public kind of what they're hearing in terms of kind of demand signals,

01:17:20.880 --> 01:17:21.760
uh, what we saw.

01:17:22.160 --> 01:17:26.400
So there's general consensus around desiring there to continue to be in,

01:17:26.480 --> 01:17:28.880
and, and expand opportunities for events.

01:17:29.230 --> 01:17:31.550
Uh, uses, uh, there in the, in the facility,

01:17:31.630 --> 01:17:34.540
both indoors, which y'all took action on with the roundhouse just,

01:17:34.630 --> 01:17:37.750
uh, at a recent meeting, uh, but then also outdoor green space.

01:17:37.830 --> 01:17:39.550
So getting more green space, trees and things,

01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:42.310
make it more park-like and create more space for,

01:17:42.430 --> 01:17:45.990
uh, for public events outdoors, uh, as well as preserving.

01:17:46.200 --> 01:17:49.040
The, the, the roundhouse as an indoor event space.

01:17:49.400 --> 01:17:51.650
Uh, we also will, there's things about the way,

01:17:51.770 --> 01:17:54.650
the way the roundhouse is used for events where there's certainly some improvements

01:17:54.650 --> 01:17:57.390
immediately around it that can improve that footprint and make that a,

01:17:57.530 --> 01:17:58.880
that a more viable space.

01:17:59.100 --> 01:18:01.700
Uh, and the action you took, uh, basically gave it,

01:18:01.890 --> 01:18:06.320
gave that building, uh, back to early works for its use to the public,

01:18:06.450 --> 01:18:07.720
uh, as an event space.

01:18:07.780 --> 01:18:09.920
And so we're, we'll continue to partner with them and that,

01:18:09.930 --> 01:18:11.140
that action's already been taken.

01:18:11.880 --> 01:18:15.220
Um, one of the things that I've seen in some of the dialogue,

01:18:15.360 --> 01:18:19.080
uh, around this particular action tonight with the railcars is a lot of

01:18:19.080 --> 01:18:22.600
folks kind of commingling the actual depot building with the discussion

01:18:22.600 --> 01:18:24.320
of the railcars, and I wanna pull those apart.

01:18:24.360 --> 01:18:26.920
That's what when I, when I say there's kind of some apples and oranges being

01:18:26.920 --> 01:18:28.710
commingled, that's what I'm talking about.

01:18:29.130 --> 01:18:32.920
The railcars are separate from what needs to happen in terms of preserving

01:18:32.920 --> 01:18:33.840
the historic building.

01:18:34.480 --> 01:18:38.440
So what a lot of, a lot of folks may not realize is that the only building

01:18:38.440 --> 01:18:43.540
that's actually on the historic register and is subject to the,

01:18:43.610 --> 01:18:47.600
the, uh, protection easement is the actual his is the depot building itself.

01:18:47.640 --> 01:18:50.470
So the kind of mustard colored building right up against,

01:18:50.560 --> 01:18:54.030
uh, Church Street there, uh, that, that old building is,

01:18:54.040 --> 01:18:55.910
is what we're talking about now.

01:18:55.920 --> 01:18:58.510
Behind it there are some a couple of smaller buildings while they are not

01:18:58.560 --> 01:18:58.880
under.

01:18:59.050 --> 01:19:01.670
The historic easement, uh, they are buildings that we,

01:19:01.800 --> 01:19:04.830
uh, intend to preserve and keep as part of,

01:19:04.840 --> 01:19:08.270
uh, part of the campus, as well as the passenger platform,

01:19:08.720 --> 01:19:11.470
uh, kind of the covered, uh, platform that goes,

01:19:11.600 --> 01:19:15.440
uh, runs to the east behind the, the depot that is also a replica of what

01:19:15.440 --> 01:19:16.440
was originally there.

01:19:16.560 --> 01:19:17.980
It's in very, very poor condition.

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:21.160
It needs to be rebuilt, but that, that is also not part of the historic

01:19:21.160 --> 01:19:22.760
easement because it's not this historic building.

01:19:22.800 --> 01:19:23.550
It's a replica.

01:19:24.000 --> 01:19:27.400
Uh, that was built at some point in the past and it now needs to be rebuilt.

01:19:27.680 --> 01:19:30.200
So those those components of the building,

01:19:30.480 --> 01:19:33.440
uh, at this point we, we would recommend they all be preserved.

01:19:33.480 --> 01:19:36.260
The actual historic building itself must be preserved,

01:19:36.520 --> 01:19:40.360
uh, and it is, uh, general services already started some basic maintenance

01:19:40.360 --> 01:19:42.100
to it, about to, you'll, you'll see that,

01:19:42.290 --> 01:19:45.240
uh, really pick up here over the next few months they are.

01:19:45.380 --> 01:19:49.410
In direct communication with SHIPO, uh, from state agency that has oversight

01:19:49.410 --> 01:19:52.200
over that, making sure that everything that we do in terms of repair,

01:19:52.490 --> 01:19:55.640
uh, is, is done appropriately and under their,

01:19:55.650 --> 01:19:56.650
under their supervision.

01:19:56.850 --> 01:19:59.470
So things that you will see in the very near future is a new roof.

01:19:59.770 --> 01:20:00.960
Uh, you'll see work going on.

01:20:00.970 --> 01:20:03.330
There's places where we're still having water infiltration of the building

01:20:03.330 --> 01:20:04.250
and getting that resolved.

01:20:04.370 --> 01:20:05.840
Ultimately it needs to be repainted.

01:20:06.170 --> 01:20:06.730
Uh, you've got a number.

01:20:07.020 --> 01:20:08.220
Windows that are in poor condition.

01:20:08.300 --> 01:20:11.500
So there's those kinds of things, maintenance of the structure itself and,

01:20:11.540 --> 01:20:13.930
and really talk about the envelope of the building.

01:20:14.140 --> 01:20:17.810
So that's one issue we've seen a lot of folks concerned about its condition

01:20:17.810 --> 01:20:20.140
and that absolutely needs to be addressed and is being addressed in General

01:20:20.140 --> 01:20:23.650
Services, uh, has that task and, and you may recall that,

01:20:23.700 --> 01:20:26.620
uh, we recently, a couple years ago actually set aside some money in the

01:20:26.620 --> 01:20:28.190
capital plan specifically.

01:20:28.450 --> 01:20:31.730
To start taking on some of those basic maintenance issues so they have the

01:20:31.730 --> 01:20:34.360
they have the funding to do those things that will that will happen here

01:20:34.360 --> 01:20:35.150
in the near term.

01:20:35.730 --> 01:20:38.850
Now that is dealing with the envelope of the building,

01:20:39.080 --> 01:20:40.790
making sure that it's properly preserved,

01:20:40.840 --> 01:20:42.560
uh, and we, and that's something that we have to do.

01:20:42.580 --> 01:20:45.120
There's also some mechanical system issues and other things that that need

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:48.920
to be resolved that is separate and distinct from the conversation around

01:20:48.920 --> 01:20:49.990
its future use.

01:20:50.760 --> 01:20:54.500
When we talk about the public meetings, we heard a kind of a wide variety

01:20:54.500 --> 01:20:57.320
of ideas thrown out by the public of things to consider.

01:20:57.730 --> 01:21:01.970
Uh, you know, some people talk about more event space or talk about potentially

01:21:01.970 --> 01:21:03.760
are there may be some commercial applications,

01:21:03.990 --> 01:21:07.250
uh, which sometimes we have some good examples of historic buildings in

01:21:07.250 --> 01:21:10.030
our community that actually have sys have remained in,

01:21:10.090 --> 01:21:13.210
in a, in a private commercial use, and that's been a good way to help preserve

01:21:13.210 --> 01:21:13.710
the building.

01:21:14.110 --> 01:21:17.870
Uh, and then we've also haul, you know, heard a number of different types

01:21:17.870 --> 01:21:20.870
of museum ideas, all right, uh, and so, uh,

01:21:21.110 --> 01:21:24.790
where we sit today, all those potential uses are on the table,

01:21:24.910 --> 01:21:29.150
and our intent would be in the near future to actually send out an RFI and

01:21:29.150 --> 01:21:29.790
start getting.

01:21:30.100 --> 01:21:33.270
Putting really kind of hanging some meat on the bones of those ideas,

01:21:33.500 --> 01:21:35.780
uh, it's easy to say, hey, let's turn it into a museum or hey,

01:21:35.820 --> 01:21:36.830
let's turn it into a restaurant.

01:21:36.940 --> 01:21:40.340
You gotta have somebody actually capable of doing that with a real viable

01:21:40.340 --> 01:21:42.880
idea, a financial plan, and all those kinds of things,

01:21:43.100 --> 01:21:45.970
uh, to see what's, what's genuinely a viable idea.

01:21:46.340 --> 01:21:51.040
What, what we have not really pursued and contemplated is trying to turn

01:21:51.040 --> 01:21:55.570
it back into a full railroad museum, uh, and there's,

01:21:55.620 --> 01:21:56.810
there's a couple of reasons for that.

01:21:56.820 --> 01:21:57.290
One is.

01:21:58.320 --> 01:21:59.870
That that's been tried and failed.

01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:04.320
Uh, I think, you know, part of the issue there is that we already have a

01:22:04.320 --> 01:22:07.910
very viable functioning active rail museum.

01:22:08.280 --> 01:22:10.360
Uh, you, you heard from a couple of representatives here tonight.

01:22:10.400 --> 01:22:12.640
There's other representatives of that organization here in the room,

01:22:13.000 --> 01:22:17.200
uh, and there's some things that they have out there at that site that that

01:22:17.200 --> 01:22:19.400
capability does not exist in the Downtown Depot.

01:22:19.480 --> 01:22:21.430
One of the things that's great about NARM,

01:22:21.720 --> 01:22:23.310
uh, is it's a living museum.

01:22:23.600 --> 01:22:25.460
You don't get to just go see.

01:22:25.860 --> 01:22:30.050
The rail cars and walk around them or walk on them and you know learn about

01:22:30.060 --> 01:22:33.780
them you actually get to ride on them sometimes it's actually it's experiential

01:22:33.780 --> 01:22:37.670
it's got some of the things that we see in modern museums uh that make it

01:22:37.670 --> 01:22:40.790
meaningful and make it attractive for people to come back over and over

01:22:40.790 --> 01:22:41.070
again.

01:22:41.530 --> 01:22:44.450
Uh, and so there's, there's some great things about NARM,

01:22:44.730 --> 01:22:48.240
uh, that, uh, that we think makes them a great partner and doing a great

01:22:48.250 --> 01:22:52.930
job of preserving rail history, uh, and teaching those next generations

01:22:52.930 --> 01:22:57.280
about the history of the present and the future of rail and its capacity

01:22:57.290 --> 01:22:57.650
in, in our.

01:22:58.110 --> 01:23:02.420
And so, uh, we don't believe that Huntsville's big enough to try and have

01:23:02.480 --> 01:23:03.650
two separate rail museums.

01:23:03.750 --> 01:23:07.120
We just don't think that's viable, uh, and financially we don't think that

01:23:07.130 --> 01:23:09.630
that would be a good pursuit of its use.

01:23:09.870 --> 01:23:14.110
There also are other some great ideas out there for that building that would.

01:23:16.520 --> 01:23:21.120
Uh, there's, uh, there's also a lot of great ideas out there or potentially

01:23:21.120 --> 01:23:24.080
great ideas, uh, that would be in direct conflict to that.

01:23:24.160 --> 01:23:26.480
Obviously some of them become mutually exclusive and you can't do,

01:23:26.600 --> 01:23:27.670
can't do all those things, but.

01:23:28.060 --> 01:23:33.130
Uh, we do not believe that trying to continue to run a rail museum at that

01:23:33.130 --> 01:23:36.220
site ultimately is viable, and we think there's other probably better uses

01:23:36.220 --> 01:23:39.660
and some of the things we heard from, from the community is other ideas

01:23:39.660 --> 01:23:42.140
that would run in conflict with that, and we think those are better things

01:23:42.140 --> 01:23:42.840
to pursue.

01:23:43.250 --> 01:23:45.520
Uh, so when we talk about more green space,

01:23:45.700 --> 01:23:48.450
more open space, more event space, all of the area,

01:23:48.820 --> 01:23:52.080
the, the portions of that acreage taken up by the railcars and things that

01:23:52.090 --> 01:23:54.730
that are sitting there and all of the asphalt that surrounds it.

01:23:55.070 --> 01:23:58.230
Uh, are, are all in conflict with that, uh,

01:23:58.360 --> 01:24:02.470
and so we believe that, uh, that's better to go to the experts at NARM and

01:24:02.470 --> 01:24:03.290
allow them to do that.

01:24:03.470 --> 01:24:06.510
I, I saw a question, you know, you know, does the city actually have staff

01:24:06.510 --> 01:24:09.070
capable of maintaining railcars?

01:24:09.110 --> 01:24:10.470
And the answer to that is no, we do not.

01:24:10.550 --> 01:24:10.880
We.

01:24:11.250 --> 01:24:13.520
In fact, uh, when, uh, they can ask, well,

01:24:13.560 --> 01:24:14.840
you know, who are the experts that we turn to,

01:24:14.880 --> 01:24:16.880
the experts we turn to are all the guys at NARM.

01:24:17.070 --> 01:24:18.780
Now it just so happens we actually have a,

01:24:18.920 --> 01:24:20.320
a person on city staff.

01:24:20.360 --> 01:24:23.580
He's not responsible in any way in his, in his city job for railcars,

01:24:23.600 --> 01:24:25.210
but he, he happens to be a member of NARM.

01:24:25.280 --> 01:24:27.360
So I sometimes go pick his brain and ask him questions.

01:24:27.850 --> 01:24:30.370
Because of expertise, but his expertise has nothing to do with his city

01:24:30.370 --> 01:24:30.490
job.

01:24:30.530 --> 01:24:34.160
It has to do with his work in NARM and the work that they do in preserving,

01:24:34.410 --> 01:24:35.620
uh, those railcars and things.

01:24:35.690 --> 01:24:40.050
And so, uh, our recommendation is that we use this as an opportunity to

01:24:40.050 --> 01:24:43.810
bolster and improve what we already have that is successful in,

01:24:43.890 --> 01:24:44.850
in the form of NARM.

01:24:45.260 --> 01:24:49.140
Now how did we go about, you know, the, the specific cars that are listed

01:24:49.140 --> 01:24:51.280
in the document, uh, that would go to NARM?

01:24:51.500 --> 01:24:52.180
How did we get to that?

01:24:52.260 --> 01:24:55.440
So first off is we gave them, uh, first dibs.

01:24:55.660 --> 01:24:58.570
We actually, they brought a group, uh, of folks out,

01:24:58.740 --> 01:25:01.820
uh, who went through the, the rail cars, identified the specific ones that

01:25:01.820 --> 01:25:03.570
they wanted in it as well as some of the other,

01:25:03.580 --> 01:25:04.690
other pieces of equipment.

01:25:05.180 --> 01:25:08.020
Uh, and so they got first dibs on that, uh,

01:25:08.140 --> 01:25:12.670
and then the other categories, uh, that we have in the document are those

01:25:12.670 --> 01:25:13.300
that were left over.

01:25:13.380 --> 01:25:17.020
They did not identify as wanting to go out to NARM and and did not think

01:25:17.020 --> 01:25:20.010
it was viable in the way they're doing the presentation at their facility,

01:25:20.310 --> 01:25:22.250
uh, so that's where, where we got to those others,

01:25:22.550 --> 01:25:25.260
um, the other thing that about the property,

01:25:25.300 --> 01:25:28.260
you know, I noted that the depot building is the only one that's under the

01:25:28.260 --> 01:25:30.100
historic preservation easement.

01:25:30.520 --> 01:25:34.720
Uh, much of what's there, uh, is not actually native to that site.

01:25:34.760 --> 01:25:35.840
It's not original to that site.

01:25:36.010 --> 01:25:37.800
A lot much of what's there, the roundhouse,

01:25:38.080 --> 01:25:41.770
you know, where the turntable is, all those things are replicas that were

01:25:42.110 --> 01:25:44.610
somewhere else, either in our community or not even in Huntsville.

01:25:44.840 --> 01:25:47.440
They're things that, so when, uh, when the decision was made many years

01:25:47.440 --> 01:25:50.600
ago to try to tell the story, uh, they brought in replicas.

01:25:50.640 --> 01:25:52.320
They brought in other things from other locations,

01:25:52.360 --> 01:25:56.480
and so, uh, those are not, there's, those are not site specific,

01:25:56.760 --> 01:25:58.960
uh, in, in terms of that particular location.

01:25:59.340 --> 01:26:01.070
So, uh, that's kind of the difference.

01:26:01.180 --> 01:26:04.460
So what I wanna make certain that people understand is that what we're presenting

01:26:04.460 --> 01:26:07.260
to you tonight has absolutely nothing to do with the depot building.

01:26:07.300 --> 01:26:10.220
We agree that probability's gotta be properly maintained and those actions

01:26:10.220 --> 01:26:12.860
are moving forward and there's nothing, there's nothing that,

01:26:12.900 --> 01:26:15.860
uh, that you have in front of you tonight that is related to the building

01:26:15.860 --> 01:26:18.360
itself, uh, so that is, uh, you know, that's a,

01:26:18.380 --> 01:26:21.580
a separate thing, uh, and I fully expect that some of the things that we'll

01:26:21.580 --> 01:26:24.620
need to do will require construction contracts or or other contracts that

01:26:24.620 --> 01:26:27.570
hit thresholds that require council approval and so as we hit those.

01:26:27.790 --> 01:26:30.860
We'll shortly be bringing those to you and presenting those publicly uh

01:26:30.860 --> 01:26:32.210
for your approval, so.

01:26:32.990 --> 01:26:34.980
So getting back to, you know, so what is,

01:26:35.140 --> 01:26:36.190
what is tonight about?

01:26:36.220 --> 01:26:39.620
It's about getting, getting the railcars to places where they can continue

01:26:39.620 --> 01:26:41.660
to be used and continue to be available to,

01:26:41.700 --> 01:26:44.550
uh, uh, to the public, uh, you know, with the,

01:26:44.570 --> 01:26:46.480
the vast majority of us going actually to the,

01:26:46.490 --> 01:26:49.970
to, to the active, uh, successful museum we have in our community in the,

01:26:49.980 --> 01:26:50.780
in the form of norm.

01:26:52.120 --> 01:26:53.500
There are two other categories.

01:26:53.870 --> 01:26:57.300
Uh, we also have, uh, what really kind of falls in the economic development

01:26:57.350 --> 01:27:00.350
category, uh, an entity intending to, uh,

01:27:00.470 --> 01:27:04.230
to build a commercial electric vehicle charging station available to the

01:27:04.230 --> 01:27:08.150
public, uh, and they've asked to be able to purchase two of the rail cars

01:27:08.150 --> 01:27:12.270
to then reinterpret into, uh, to uses there on that.

01:27:12.280 --> 01:27:14.790
And so they would ultimately be accessible to the public through use of

01:27:14.790 --> 01:27:17.740
that, that commercial, uh, purpose, but they would be sold to them.

01:27:18.340 --> 01:27:21.470
Uh, and then there's an additional one that was a little bit unusual.

01:27:21.540 --> 01:27:25.100
We had a citizen reach out to us and ask to be able to purchase it.

01:27:25.460 --> 01:27:28.380
Uh, we, what we talked to her about is we're not,

01:27:28.460 --> 01:27:29.740
we can't just sell one to you.

01:27:29.780 --> 01:27:31.740
We need to, we need to make it available to the public.

01:27:31.940 --> 01:27:35.380
And so with your approval, that particular one would allow us to put out

01:27:35.380 --> 01:27:40.260
an RFP and competitively, uh, put it out available for sale and not just

01:27:40.260 --> 01:27:43.670
give it to one person but actually, uh, sell it competitively.

01:27:43.940 --> 01:27:45.970
And so that's that third category that you see there,

01:27:45.980 --> 01:27:47.700
that one, that one last car.

01:27:49.080 --> 01:27:49.410
